    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length",
    "problem": "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s={e}^{t}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}-1 & =s \\\\ {e}^{t} & =s+1 \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-(s+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
